Here is a small example:

```java
class Animal {
    String speak() { return "..."; }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    String speak() { return "Woof"; }
}
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Dog();
        System.out.println(a.speak());
    }
}
```

The variable `a` has type `Animal` but refers to a `Dog`, so `a.speak()` runs the `Dog` version and prints `Woof`.

**Check:** What would be printed if `a` held `new Animal()` instead?
